+# Implementation Plan: S004 Provider retry quota
+
+**Branch**: `S004-provider-retry` | **Date**: 2026-08-11 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)
+**Input**: Story specification from `specs/projects/001-pulseboard/epics/E002-reminders/stories/S004-provider-retry/spec.md`
+
+## Execution Flow
+
+Planning is complete through Phase 1. `/story-tasks` will turn the design and test scenarios below into the ordered implementation checklist; it must not create a separate reservation and refund implementation.
+
+## Summary
+
+Provide one transactional retry-credit lifecycle for each reminder send attempt. The lifecycle reserves a single workspace credit before provider dispatch, settles that reservation on provider acceptance, and releases it for provider failure, timeout, or cancellation. Stable attempt and callback identities make every terminal transition idempotent, while the reservation write prevents concurrent sends from overspending the quota.
+
+## Technical Approach
+
+Introduce a retry-credit reservation record owned by a workspace and a reminder-send attempt. The attempt begins by atomically decrementing only when the available balance is positive and inserting (or reusing) its reservation in one storage transaction. Provider outcomes call one settlement operation that changes a pending reservation to `finalized` or `released`; the update is conditional on the pending state, so repeated callbacks have no further balance effect. The provider invocation happens after a durable reservation exists, and cancellation/timeout use the same release path as an explicit provider failure. No source code, persistence schema, framework, or endpoint conventions exist in this workspace, so `/story-tasks` must attach these operations to the established application seams rather than introduce a parallel quota subsystem.
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- Requires: a workspace-scoped retry-credit balance and a reminder-send attempt identity in the host application; neither implementation is present in this workspace.
+- Requires: the provider adapter to expose a stable attempt/callback identity and to route accept, fail, timeout, and cancellation outcomes into the settlement operation.
+- Provides: an idempotent, non-negative quota boundary for later reminder retry delivery work.
+
+## Testing Strategy
+
+- Unit tests: reservation state transitions; conditional settlement; duplicate success, failure, timeout, and cancellation calls; invalid or missing identities.
+- Integration tests: two concurrent attempts against one available credit; provider acceptance; each unsuccessful outcome; a callback replay after each terminal outcome.
+- Contract tests: the service contract in [contracts/provider-retry-quota.yaml](contracts/provider-retry-quota.yaml), including required identifiers and the invariant that a terminal callback cannot mutate the balance twice.
+
+## Technical Context
+
+**Language/Version**: No application source is present; use the host application's established language and version.  
+**Primary Dependencies**: Existing persistence transaction facility and provider adapter; no new dependency is planned.  
+**Storage**: Existing durable workspace quota store, extended with a durable reservation ledger.  
+**Testing**: Existing host test runner; tests must execute against a transaction-capable persistence boundary, not an in-memory mock alone.  
+**Target Platform**: Existing backend reminder-delivery runtime.  
+**Project Type**: Backend service; this story has no UI.  
+**Performance Goals**: Preserve the provider dispatch path while making the reservation write a single bounded transaction; no numeric target is specified.  
+**Constraints**: Workspace isolation; balance never negative; exactly-once balance effect per attempt despite at-least-once callbacks; failure paths restore exactly one credit.  
+**Scale/Scope**: One workspace quota and one reservation per send attempt; concurrent attempts and replayed callbacks are in scope.
+
+## Constitution Check
+
+No project or epic constitution, domain model, UX strategy, design system, journey, wireframes, outline, codebase scan, or prior research report exists in this workspace. The following gates are derived directly from the story specification and are binding for this plan:
+
+- **Quota integrity**: reserve only when a workspace has positive available credit, and perform the balance mutation and reservation creation atomically.
+- **Outcome symmetry**: provider failure, timeout, cancellation, and rollback all reach the same idempotent release transition; none may be deferred to another story.
+- **Idempotency**: a stable attempt identity and conditional pending-to-terminal transition ensure duplicate callbacks cannot settle twice.
+- **Testability**: concurrency and callback replay require integration coverage at the real transaction boundary.
+- **UX and brand**: not applicable; this is a backend-only story with no user-facing copy or screens.
+
+Initial Constitution Check: PASS. The design below preserves all gates without a new external dependency or a cross-service abstraction. Post-Design Constitution Check: PASS.
+
+## Architecture Decision Gate
+
+This story has a detailed architecture gate because balance integrity is concurrent and provider callbacks are duplicable. The workspace contains no implementation evidence, so this is a reversible semantic decision rather than a lock to a database or framework.
+
+| Alternative | Decision | Reason |
+|---|---|---|
+| Decrement balance before dispatch, increment on every failure callback | Rejected | A duplicated failure callback can over-credit and a crash can strand the repair path. |
+| Keep an in-memory per-workspace lock and balance | Rejected | It cannot survive process restart or coordinate multiple workers. |
+| Durable reservation ledger plus conditional atomic state transitions | Chosen | It provides one auditable owner for a credit, supports replay-safe settlement, and works with concurrent workers. |
+
+The chosen model introduces only a reservation record and operations around existing quota and attempt concepts. It does not prescribe a repository layer, event bus, or provider API until source evidence exists.
